    'body' => <<<'HTML'
<p>The driveway apron is the entrance section of a drive, where your private driveway joins the public road. "Apron" is a US term; in the UK the part that crosses the pavement or grass verge is usually called the dropped kerb or vehicle crossover. It is easy to overlook, yet it is one of the hardest-working surfaces on the property. It takes the weight of delivery vans and bin lorries, the salt and slush thrown up from the road in winter, and the rainwater running along the gutter line.</p>
<p>For many homeowners the apron is an afterthought until a crack appears, water starts pooling near the garage or the council or city gets in touch about non-compliant work. Replacing or repairing it is a significant job, and doing it wrong can mean paying for it twice, along with any enforcement or damage that follows. Here are the most common, and most costly, mistakes and how to avoid them.</p>

<h2 id="local-regulations">1. Ignoring who owns it and what the rules say</h2>
<p>One of the most expensive mistakes happens before anyone picks up a spade: not finding out who owns the apron and what the local rules require.</p>
<p>In the UK, the section that crosses the pavement or verge is usually highway land. You need consent from the council (as highway authority) to create, widen or rebuild a dropped kerb, and many councils insist that the work is done by their own approved contractor or carry it out themselves. Your own responsibility normally starts at your boundary. In the US, the apron often sits in the public right of way, even where the homeowner pays for it; cities typically require a right-of-way or encroachment permit, publish their own specifications for width, flares, slope and construction, and sometimes ask for a bond. Who maintains it afterwards varies from place to place.</p>
<p>Councils and cities often specify a thicker, reinforced construction for the crossing than for the rest of the drive, because refuse lorries and other heavy vehicles may run over it. Your local specification sets the details. Work that does not meet it, or was done without consent, can be rejected and may have to be taken out and redone at your expense. Check with your local highway authority or public works department before you plan anything, and get the answer in writing.</p>

<h2 id="soil-preparation">2. Skimping on the ground underneath</h2>
<p>How long an apron lasts depends largely on what lies beneath it. A common shortcut is poor preparation of the subgrade, the natural ground the apron sits on. Turf, roots and topsoil must be dug out, because organic material rots and leaves voids.</p>
<p>If the ground is not properly compacted with a vibrating plate or roller, it settles unevenly under traffic, and the result is sinking sections or the web of cracks often called alligator cracking. A well-built apron sits on a layer of compacted crushed stone that spreads the load and gives a stable platform. Its depth depends on your soil and the specification for the crossing, so ask your contractor to state it on the quote. Saving on the base is false economy; it is the part you cannot fix later without digging the whole apron up.</p>

<h2 id="drainage-management">3. Getting the drainage wrong</h2>
<p>Water is the main enemy of any paved surface. An apron with the wrong fall lets water stand at the road edge or, worse, sends it back up the drive towards the garage and the house. Water that gets under the surface softens the base, lifts concrete and breaks up asphalt.</p>
<p>The apron needs to keep its fall towards the road and the gutter or drainage channel, as your local specification and installer set it. Where the property sits lower than the road, a channel (trench) drain across the drive, rated for vehicle loads, or another drainage solution may be needed to stop water running towards the building; in some places a pipe under the entrance keeps a roadside ditch flowing, and that usually needs the highway authority's agreement. In England, also remember that a new or replacement hard surface over 5 m² in a front garden generally needs planning permission unless it is permeable or drains to a permeable area. Check with your local authority, and ask your contractor to confirm the levels with a laser or optical level rather than by eye.</p>

<!--figure:clogged-drain-rainy-street|An apron that sheds water towards a blocked drain soon floods the pavement.-->

<h2 id="material-choice">4. Choosing the wrong material</h2>
<p>Choosing a surface on upfront price alone is a frequent mistake. Asphalt (tarmac) is usually cheaper to lay, but at the entrance it takes the scuffing of tyres turning tightly, which can scar and loosen the surface over time.</p>
<p>Many homeowners choose concrete for the apron even where the rest of the drive is asphalt, because it resists rutting from heavy vehicles. If you do, make sure the mix is specified for an external crossing in your climate; in places with hard frosts, contractors commonly use an air-entrained mix to help the surface resist freeze-thaw scaling. Block paving or setts look handsome, but at the entrance they need an edge restraint and a construction to suit the loads, or they can creep and sink. On highway land, the material may not be your choice at all, as many councils and cities specify it.</p>

<h2 id="expansion-joints">5. Leaving out the joints</h2>
<p>Concrete expands in heat and shrinks in cold. Leaving out isolation (expansion) joints, or putting them in the wrong place, means the apron pushes against the kerb, the road or the drive behind it, and relieves the stress by cracking where it likes.</p>
<p>Flexible joint strips usually go where the apron meets the kerb or road edge and where it joins the main driveway, so each part can move independently. Control joints, the grooves cut or tooled into the surface, give the slab a planned place to crack. Their depth and spacing should be set by the contractor for your slab, or by the local specification on highway land; joints that are too shallow or too far apart are a common cause of random, unsightly cracks.</p>

<h2 id="utility-clearance">6. Digging without checking for utilities</h2>
<p>The ground at the entrance often carries water, gas, electricity and telecoms cables, because services usually run under the pavement or verge. Digging without locating them is dangerous and can make you liable for the repair.</p>
<p>In the US, call 811 before you dig so that buried lines can be marked. In the UK, get the utility companies' plans and have the area scanned with a cable avoidance tool before excavation; this is also one reason councils prefer their own contractors on highway land. Just as important, do not pave over or bury utility access covers or stopcock boxes (in the US, curb boxes or shut-off valves). If a utility company needs to reach a pipe it cannot find, it may break out your new surface to get to it, and the reinstatement may not match.</p>

<h2 id="professional-installation">7. DIY or the lowest bid</h2>
<p>A patio is a reasonable weekend project; an apron usually is not. It needs the right equipment, careful timing and, often, an approved contractor. Concrete sets on its own schedule, and if the finishing is mistimed or the weather turns, the surface can be spoilt.</p>
<p>Choosing a contractor on the lowest price is another trap. A much cheaper quote may mean less reinforcement, a thinner slab, a shallower base or no proper curing, and a badly finished or poorly cured surface can start to dust and flake early. Look for a contractor with suitable insurance, recent local apron or crossover work you can see, and, where required, approval from your council or city. Ask for an itemised quote that states the base, thickness, reinforcement, joints and drainage. Once the concrete is down, follow the contractor's advice on curing and on when you can drive on it.</p>

<h2 id="ongoing-maintenance">8. Treating it as finished once it is laid</h2>
<p>An apron is not "set and forget". On the part that is yours, an asphalt surface benefits from a sealer made for asphalt, applied every few years as the label advises, and concrete from a penetrating sealer that helps keep salt out. Avoid coal-tar sealers, which are restricted in some areas. Keep the joints filled with a flexible sealant so water cannot reach the base.</p>
<p>Weeds are a slow, quiet cause of damage: their roots open joints and cracks. Pull them out early, and if you use a weedkiller, follow the label and keep it away from road gullies, ponds and watercourses. Small, regular jobs cost far less than rebuilding a failed entrance. For a routine through the year, see our <a href="/blog/driveway-apron-maintenance-by-season">seasonal driveway apron maintenance</a> guide.</p>
<blockquote><p><strong>Tip:</strong> Before work starts, take dated photos of the kerb, pavement or verge, and any covers and boxes at the entrance. If there is a later dispute about damage or who is responsible, you will have a clear record of how it looked.</p></blockquote>

<h2 id="faq">FAQ</h2>
<h3>Who is responsible for repairing a driveway apron?</h3>
<p>It depends where you live. In the UK, the dropped kerb or crossover on the pavement or verge is usually highway land, so report damage there to the council rather than repairing it yourself; your part normally starts at your boundary. In the US, the homeowner is often responsible even though the apron sits in the public right of way, but policies vary, so check with your city or county.</p>
<h3>How thick should a concrete driveway apron be?</h3>
<p>Thicker than a path, and often thicker than the rest of the drive. Councils and cities often specify a thicker, reinforced construction for the crossing; your local specification sets it. On your own land, your contractor or an engineer should specify it for your soil and the vehicles that will use it.</p>
<h3>Why is my driveway apron cracking near the road?</h3>
<p>The usual causes are the weight of vehicles turning in from the road, water getting under the slab and freezing (frost heave), poor compaction of the ground underneath, or missing joints. A contractor can tell you which, and whether the fix is a repair or a rebuild.</p>
<h3>Can I use salt on my apron in winter?</h3>
<p>Avoid rock salt on new concrete and near metal such as channel drains, and avoid ammonium-based de-icers, which can attack concrete. Choose a de-icer your installer or the surface manufacturer approves and use as little as works. Sand gives grip without chemicals; sweep it up in spring.</p>
<h3>How long before I can drive on a new apron?</h3>
<p>Follow your contractor's advice. For concrete, cars usually wait at least a week and heavier vehicles longer. New asphalt can usually take traffic sooner, but it stays soft in hot weather for a while, so avoid turning the wheels on the spot or parking in the same place.</p>

<h2 id="final-thoughts">Final thoughts</h2>
<p>Most apron problems come back to a few early decisions: getting consent and following the local specification, building a sound base, keeping water flowing towards the road, choosing a suitable material, putting the joints in the right places and protecting buried services. Get those right and keep up with the small jobs, and the entrance should serve you well. If you are budgeting for a new one, read <a href="/blog/driveway-apron-cost">what drives the cost of a driveway apron</a>, and for the drive behind it, see the common <a href="/blog/concrete-driveway-mistakes">concrete driveway mistakes</a>. For help designing the front garden around your entrance, explore our <a href="/services/balcony-terrace-decoration">balcony and terrace decoration</a> service or <a href="/book-consultation">book a consultation</a>.</p>
